menu "MR-NavQ95 board options"

choice
	prompt "GPIO_IO23 (PWM header J9 pin 9)"
	default BOARD_NAVQ95_IO23_PWM
	---help---
		The pad is TPM6 channel 1. As PWM output 8 it is the fourth output
		group, PWM or DShot like the other seven. As the buzzer it drives the
		GPS connector buzzer line (J8 pin 9) through the tone alarm driver;
		switch S1 must be closed for the buzzer to sound. Both need the
		System Manager configuration that assigns LPTPM6 to the M7, which
		the NavQ95 BSP provides. Unused leaves the pad and TPM6 untouched,
		for a System Manager configuration without that assignment.

config BOARD_NAVQ95_IO23_PWM
	bool "PWM output 8"

config BOARD_NAVQ95_IO23_BUZZER
	bool "Buzzer"
	select DRIVERS_TONE_ALARM

config BOARD_NAVQ95_IO23_NONE
	bool "Unused"
endchoice

choice
	prompt "LPUART2 (/dev/ttyS1, M7 console port)"
	default BOARD_NAVQ95_LPUART2_RC
	---help---
		LPUART2 is the M7 console port of the board: it reaches the CP2105
		USB serial bridge on the IO board (J1 CONSOLE, second port, ttyUSB1
		on a Linux host) and the main board J2 CONSOLE header (pins 4 and 5).
		As RC input it carries the RC protocol (CRSF, SBUS, ...) instead,
		with the receiver on J2 pins 4 and 5; do not connect a PC to ttyUSB1
		then, as its transmitter shares the line with the receiver. As NSH
		console it runs an NSH shell started after boot on that port, a
		plain USB serial console that does not need the Linux side; the
		NuttX system console itself stays on the RPMsg tty. UART2 TX is also
		the BT_MODE1 boot strap, so nothing may drive that line while the
		board resets.

config BOARD_NAVQ95_LPUART2_RC
	bool "RC input"

config BOARD_NAVQ95_LPUART2_CONSOLE
	bool "NSH console"
endchoice

config BOARD_SERIAL_RC
	default "/dev/ttyS1" if BOARD_NAVQ95_LPUART2_RC

config BOARD_ADDITIONAL_INIT
	default "rc.lpuart2_console" if BOARD_NAVQ95_LPUART2_CONSOLE

endmenu
